==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|corajous}}, from {{der|en|fro|corageus}}, from {{m|fro|corage}} + {{m|fro|-eus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈɹeɪd͡ʒəs/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-courageous.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈɹæɪd͡ʒəs/|a=AU,NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪdʒəs|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of a person, displaying or possessing [[courage]]. {{C|en|Personality}}
#: {{syn|en|bold|brave|hardy|valorous}}
#: {{ux|en|the most '''courageous''' person I have ever met}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2005
|author=w:Jesse Helms
|chapter=Foreign Relations Experiences
|title=Here's Where I Stand: A Memoir
|url=https://archive.org/details/hereswhereistand0000helm/
|location=[[New York]]
|publisher=w:Random House
|ISBN=0-375-50884-8
|LCCN=2005042795
|OCLC=835465798
|pages=228–229
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/hereswhereistand0000helm/page/228/
|text=In 1979, following Jimmy Carter’s unexpected and unreasonable decision to transfer diplomatic recognition from Taipei to Beijing, the Congress passed the Taiwan Relations Act to preserve our relationship with this '''courageous''' nation. We believed that it was vital for our allies and for those who were not our allies to know that the United States would not abandon its friends.}}
# Of an action, that requires courage.
#: {{syn|en|bold|brave|heroic}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''courageous''' deed}}

